Z||000000||AusFiero||07-27-2001||10:05 AM||||Well I must be bored. I dug a picture of my old Subaru WRX turbo. God it was a fun car to drive.<BR>Permanent 4X4. Turbocharged 210 KW (280hp) of all out driving fun. It was just so forgiving. Shame it just wasn't practical as a family car at the time, and was way too expensive to insure and maintain. Only kept it 6 months and it was a case of lose the Fiero or lose the Subaru. I just love my Fiero too much to part with it I think.<BR>You do have these in the states don't you? <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wrx01.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||6
Z||000001||TRiAD||07-27-2001||10:12 AM||||Yeah, we just got them this year, and they only have 227HP here, for now...We're being told in 2 ears the "STi" version (280HP) should arrive. They are also all 4 door sedans here...no coupes. <P>We also never got the Nissan "Sunny" Pulsar GTiR, the Celica GTFour (we had a 200HP AWD "AllTrac" for a couple years, but not after '92 or so), Nissan Skyline (although MotoRex imports them now), and several others. <P>Nice cars, though! can't wait 'till the new Lancer gets here this year and the real battles begin! We should have a few competitors in this class within 4-5 years...<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||firstfiero||07-27-2001||10:30 AM||||80 bmw 528i. Never had a car let alone a sedan that could handle like that. An asolute incredible machine!<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||07-27-2001||10:59 AM||||My 56 Velam Isetta , Haven't driven it in 5 years ,it has been in a friends motorcycle shop getting a new motor.<P>Wish I had a pic of her , she is rare. Not a BMW , mine is one of less than 100 made. Looks similar to a BMW but has 2 rear wheels. 3 spd manual tranny.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-27-2001||03:48 PM||||My dad has two Isetta's, both BMW and both with dual rear wheels. One coupe and one sedan. Both 60's I believe. Strange cars, but my parents drove the heck out of them.<P>Shifter on the left in a pocket. Canvas sunroofs.<P>How were the Velam's different from the BMW'?<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||07-27-2001||04:17 PM||||No idea , got mine from my grandfather in   the late 80's when he passed away , he got the car in 1961 with a broken crank , adapted a snowmobile motor to it. The rings in the snomobile motor went it was 300cc. 3spd , has a canvas slider sunroof. Now has a 650 cc kawi motor and tranny in it , it's been a long slow project getting it put in. It's only ever been on the road 5 or so years of it's life. Was found in northern saskatchewan by my grandfather.<P>it has seperate headlights , more of an egg shape to it  no body lines on the side. It's impossible to find parts for it so were kinda having fun with it. <P>I saw an article in a early 70 Hot rod book that had a corvair motor in an isetta..it was quite funny ,they made a secion to completley cover the motor , did a real nice job. it would lift the wheels all over the place. I imagine with the kawasaki motor I will be able to pulla few wheel stands<P>JM ||||reg||1
